Finally, a wearable for your vagina

There are, what feels like, a million wearables on the market that track ev=
erything from glucose levels to sleep. Yet, until recently, there was nothi=
ng to address the one bloody thing that half of the world=E2=80=99s populat=
ion deals with on a monthly basis: periods.

That's why Emm founder Jenny Button =E2=80=94 who, in 2020, found herself w=
anting Oura ring-style insights about her menstrual cycle but no products a=
vailable to provide them =E2=80=94 worked with engineers and biomedical sci=
entists to make one.

Five years and thousands of prototypes later, she debuted the world=E2=80=
=99s first smart menstrual cup, which recently raised $9m and already has a=
 30k-person waitlist, per (https://techcrunch.com/2025/11/18/emm-raises-9m-=
seed-to-create-to-launch-one-of-the-worlds-first-smart-menstrual-cups/?_hse=
nc=3Dp2ANqtz--LeuOIVf1_Mya8lb5eOo5PwCRbWPQsCnR5vizvuSllkr3i4XM1wGvnuIo-NJbF=
ne3HwQwKQcd_b6fGtoKZMX3gCuUloA&_hsmi=3D395307387 ) TechCrunch.

How it works

Like other menstrual cups, it=E2=80=99s designed to store blood, except it=
=E2=80=99s made from medical-grade silicone and embedded with ultrathin bio=
sensors that collect data (https://www.emm.co/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--LeuOIVf1_M=
ya8lb5eOo5PwCRbWPQsCnR5vizvuSllkr3i4XM1wGvnuIo-NJbFne3HwQwKQcd_b6fGtoKZMX3g=
CuUloA&_hsmi=3D395307387#how-it-works ) on the wearer=E2=80=99s menstrual c=
ycle patterns, like:

- Daily flow rate

- Cup usage, so you know when to change it

- Period and cycle length, so users can track changes over time

That data =E2=80=94 fully encrypted and anonymized =E2=80=94 is sent to Emm=
=E2=80=99s app, where users receive personal insights about their menstrual=
 health.

The cup offers 4x the capacity of a regular tampon and, when not in use, ca=
n be easily cleaned with soap and stored in its charging case for the month=
 ahead.

The hope=E2=80=A6

=E2=80=A6 is to =E2=80=9Ctransform the research, diagnosis and treatment of=
 menstrual and reproductive health conditions,=E2=80=9D Button told TechCru=
nch.

- For example: endometriosis, a painful and often misdiagnosed reproductive=
 condition that can take up to 10 years (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/artic=
les/PMC11625652/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--LeuOIVf1_Mya8lb5eOo5PwCRbWPQsCnR5vizvuSl=
lkr3i4XM1wGvnuIo-NJbFne3HwQwKQcd_b6fGtoKZMX3gCuUloA&_hsmi=3D395307387 ) to =
diagnose, largely due to a lack of data.

Emm wants to accelerate those diagnoses and give women a better, data-backe=
d understanding of their bodies.

The product launches in the UK next year, with a US expansion planned for 2=
027.

Period wearables=E2=80=A6

=E2=80=A6 represent a new but burgeoning sector within femtech, an industry=
 worth an estimated ~$40B in 2024 and expected (https://www.grandviewresear=
ch.com/industry-analysis/femtech-market-report?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--LeuOIVf1_M=
ya8lb5eOo5PwCRbWPQsCnR5vizvuSllkr3i4XM1wGvnuIo-NJbFne3HwQwKQcd_b6fGtoKZMX3g=
CuUloA&_hsmi=3D395307387 ) to grow to $97B by 2030.

And while Emm is a pioneer in the field, it=E2=80=99s not the only one work=
ing on smart solutions to address that time of the month.

- In October, researchers at Canada=E2=80=99s McMaster University released =
a seaweed-powered (https://interestingengineering.com/science/smart-menstru=
al-cup-health-monitoring?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--LeuOIVf1_Mya8lb5eOo5PwCRbWPQsCnR=
5vizvuSllkr3i4XM1wGvnuIo-NJbFne3HwQwKQcd_b6fGtoKZMX3gCuUloA&_hsmi=3D3953073=
87 ) menstrual cup that it hopes to eventually outfit with sensors to detec=
t infections and blood-borne illnesses.

- In June, scientists in Switzerland unveiled a pad-attachable device (http=
s://www.livescience.com/health/new-menstrual-pad-device-tracks-period-blood=
-for-signs-of-disease?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--LeuOIVf1_Mya8lb5eOo5PwCRbWPQsCnR5vi=
zvuSllkr3i4XM1wGvnuIo-NJbFne3HwQwKQcd_b6fGtoKZMX3gCuUloA&_hsmi=3D395307387 =
) that can detect disease biomarkers in period blood.

